  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Whitcomb broke the tie in the eleventh, but Eldridge made sure they had one to break in the first place.",
    "what_this_means": "Webb held St. Louis to one run across six innings, then the bullpen navigated the back end without surrender. Eldridge delivered three RBI in four at-bats, keeping the offense relevant through a night of thin margins. They left three runners in scoring position over fifteen plate appearances, converted just three, and still found a way through to extra innings tied at four. That's the math of a narrow escape: good enough pitching, functional hitting, and a moment that arrived late enough to feel like reprieve instead of inevitability. Whitcomb walked it off in the eleventh with a single. This is a team that doesn't win with cushion. They win because the last swing comes before the last pitch.",
    "what_to_watch": "Quinn Mathews carries a 4.38 ERA into tonight's rematch. The Cardinals' lineup has not found consistent footing against Roupp's approach. Expect another grinding affair where margin is measured in fractions and late at-bats carry everything.",
